set -e

# Check what is being committed, not the whole working tree.
#
# This used to be `biome ci .`, which lints every file it can see — including files that are
# not staged and never will be: a scratch note, a half-written file, another lane's staging
# directory. Any of those failing a format check blocked the commit of unrelated, correct
# work, and the only way out was `--no-verify`, which skips the check on the staged files
# too. A hook that is wrong about when it fires trains people to bypass the gate it is.
#
# `--no-errors-on-unmatched` is load-bearing, not tidiness. `biome check --staged` exits
# non-zero with "No files were processed in the specified paths" when the staged set holds
# nothing biome handles, which is enough to refuse a legitimate commit.
#
# Filtering the file list ourselves was tried first and was wrong in three directions at
# once, each reproduced:
#   - a hand-written extension list omits files biome handles (`.mts`), so an unformatted
#     staged `.mts` was committed with the hook silently passing it;
#   - it disagrees with `biome.json`'s own exclusions (`package.json`, `tsconfig*.json`,
#     `routeTree.gen.ts`, `openapi.json`, `**/*.css`), so it *refused* a dependency bump —
#     staged `package.json` and `pnpm-lock.yaml` alone, one of the commonest commit shapes
#     in this repository (2 of the last 292 commits);
#   - `git diff --cached --name-only` quotes unusual paths, so a non-ASCII filename failed
#     the guard and disabled the check entirely.
# Letting biome decide what it handles removes the list, the disagreement and the false
# positive together.
#
# `biome ci` has no `--staged` flag (biome 2.5.7: "Error: no such flag: `--staged`, did you
# mean `--changed`?"), and `--changed` is the wrong shape here — it diffs against a merge
# base, so by its own help text "staged and unstaged changes are not included" and it would
# check nothing at commit time.
#
# Scope, stated precisely because it is a real limit: `--staged` takes the file *list* from
# the index but reads file *contents* from the working tree. A file staged with one content
# and then edited on disk is checked as it is on disk, not as it will be committed. That is
# inherited from `biome ci .`, not introduced here, and it is why CI still runs the
# whole-checkout `pnpm lint:ci` (`biome ci .`) as the actual gate: a fresh checkout has
# neither untracked files nor a divergent working tree, so it checks the committed bytes.
pnpm exec biome check --staged --no-errors-on-unmatched

